HanPit adaptation: authoritative GF0025-2021 vocabulary Pinyin replaces
hanzi-based polyphone inference; explicit tone/neutral/erhua markings are
retained, and contextual surface sandhi is applied separately.
The Python generator contains the adapter; upstream packages are unmodified.

Phonetic policy:
- Use the first slash-separated documented reading: 谁 shei2, 熟 shu2.
- Segment marked source Pinyin, preserving apostrophe/space/hyphen boundaries.
  Tone marks determine each citation tone; unmarked syllables use neutral5.
  Hanzi heteronyms may resolve syllable boundaries, never replace source reading.
- Normalize ü/v into the v1.1 phoneme alphabet, including apical zi/ci/si
  versus zhi/chi/shi/ri finals and expanded iu/ui/un finals.
- Explicit attached r (e.g. 女孩儿 nv3 hai2+r) becomes the model R suffix.
  Explicit ér remains a full syllable (e.g. 女儿 nv3 er2).
- Use Misaki/PaddleSpeech contextual third-tone rules with deterministic jieba
  segmentation and boundary merging; do not globally flatten third-tone chains.
- 不 before a citation fourth tone becomes second tone. A不A/A一A middles
  are neutral. 一 becomes second tone before fourth, fourth before first,
  second or third; ordinal 第一 and numeric sequences retain citation tone.
- Preserve source lexical neutral tones; do not apply a dictionary that would
  silently override a deliberately marked source tone.
- These are isolated-word clips, not sentence-level prosody; deterministic
  segmentation is an approximation of natural phrasal tone-sandhi grouping.
  No human auditory validation is claimed.

Encoding: MP3, libmp3lame, 64kbps, 24000Hz, mono.
Identical final phoneme strings may share generated audio, copied to every
individual official ID. Distinct readings are never cached by hanzi alone.
Each MP3 is fully decoded for finite samples, meaningful duration and RMS.
manifest.json records per-ID citation/surface phonemes, SHA256, duration,
RMS and byte count, source hashes, dependency versions and input hashes.
Resume requires matching source/settings/script/vocabulary fingerprints and
checksums; stale outputs are not silently skipped.

